'''bent<sup>2</sup>''' (bent), ''n.'' [Also dial. ''bennet''; < ME. ''bent'', < AS. *''beonet'' (found only in comp., in local names, as in ''Beonetleáh'', > E. ''Bentley'') = OS. *''binet'' (not authenticated) = LG. ''behnd'' (Brem. Wörterb.) = OHG. ''binuz'', ''binez'', MHG. ''binz'', G. ''binse'', a bent, rush; origin unknown.] '''1.''' Any stiff or wiry grass, such as grows on commons or neglected ground. {{smaller|The name is given to many species, as ''Agrostis vulgaris'', ''Agropyrum junceum'', species of ''Aira'', etc.; in America it is applied exclusively to ''Agrostis vulgaris'' and ''A. canina.'' Also ''bent-grass.''}}

'''2.''' The culm or stalk of bent; a stalk of coarse withered grass; a dead stem of grass which has borne seed.

{{smaller block|His spear a ''bent'' both stiff and strong.
{{right|''Drayton'', Nymphidia.}}}}

'''3.''' A place covered with grass; a field; uninclosed pasture-land; a heath.

{{smaller block|Vche beste to the ''bent'' that that bytes on erbeȝ.
{{right|''Alliterative Poems'' (ed. Morris), ii. 532.}}}}

